<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish Inquisition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1478–1834</span> · Also known as Tribunal of the Holy Office of the Inquisition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> was a church tribunal under royal control, authorised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> in 1478 at the request of Isabella I and Ferdinand II. Its first targets were converted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> suspected of secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>; it later pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a>, Protestants and other dissenters across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and its empire. Under inquisitors such as Tomás de Torquemada it held public autos-da-fé, and it was finally abolished in 1834.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition.jpg" alt="Spanish Inquisition" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Di (they-them) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inquisici%C3%B3n_espa%C3%B1ola.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
